WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:04.500
Film Courage: At what point in your career
did you go without work for was it a year?

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:10.890
What happened? Erica Wernick, Personal Coach,
Author and Speaker: That was in 2009 and that

00:00:10.890 --> 00:00:16.860
contact, that one connection that I had made
helped me get that first show. But I didn't

00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:23.900
have like a huge rolodex of people that I
knew in the industry so it was really challenging

00:00:23.900 --> 00:00:31.340
to book another job because as from a graphic
designer standpoint you really need to know

00:00:31.340 --> 00:00:35.020
people. Once you get into the union there
is an availability list that you can put yourself

00:00:35.020 --> 00:00:39.620
on and you can get some jobs from your name
being on the availability list but people

00:00:39.620 --> 00:00:46.989
are only going to that list until they've
exhausted their list of people they know,

00:00:46.989 --> 00:00:52.629
so it's so much about who you know and I just
didn't know enough people yet, my pool was

00:00:52.629 --> 00:01:00.719
so small. It was like the worst year of my
life because it was also the recession and

00:01:00.719 --> 00:01:06.689
so I couldn't even get a waitressing job.
I remember this one day where I saw a Craigslist

00:01:06.689 --> 00:01:12.299
posting for a waitress interview a server
interview and I got in my car right away and

00:01:12.299 --> 00:01:15.710
drove right over and there were already like
10 people in line and they were only taking

00:01:15.710 --> 00:01:23.759
one person so that was really challenging
too and even now I try to get better but I

00:01:23.759 --> 00:01:29.680
tend to tie myself worth to how much money
I have and so when I like couldn't even make

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:36.569
a living as like getting a server job or something
like that or any kind of side job um it and

00:01:36.569 --> 00:01:40.849
and this was also before Uber and Lyft existed
you know I couldn't drive for Instacart I

00:01:40.849 --> 00:01:46.799
couldn't i couldn't do those things it couldn't
be a Postmates person um and so that was really

00:01:46.799 --> 00:01:52.279
hard for me too so not only like struggling
to find the work but then time my worth to

00:01:52.279 --> 00:01:59.549
that and and feeling so unworthy of success
or anything that i wanted that was really

00:01:59.549 --> 00:02:05.659
hard how well do you remember the depression
that you faced oh gosh i very well because

00:02:05.659 --> 00:02:10.509
i still experience it all the time like i'm
i think i'm prone to depression i call it

00:02:10.509 --> 00:02:15.959
situational depression because it usually
comes from a situation so something happens

00:02:15.959 --> 00:02:22.540
um to cause me to be depressed i don't normally
feel it just to feel it with no trigger um

00:02:22.540 --> 00:02:30.220
so i recall it very well because i felt it
again many times since then um but it was

00:02:30.220 --> 00:02:36.819
just a really pivotal time in my life like
i remember it so well so well i can really

00:02:36.819 --> 00:02:44.409
connect you know to those feelings and you
know what i tried to do you know just all

00:02:44.409 --> 00:02:48.920
the things that i did and even though it was
almost 13 years ago at this point 12 years

00:02:48.920 --> 00:02:54.340
ago i i still remember it very clearly yeah
it was a really rough time for a lot of people

00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:58.900
yes it was and i mean thank god i didn't have
kids or didn't have a home you know so many

00:02:58.900 --> 00:03:03.069
people lost their homes and you know much
worse than you know fortunately i was able

00:03:03.069 --> 00:03:08.980
to stay afloat and you know i had two roommates
at the time and so i was able to still stay

00:03:08.980 --> 00:03:14.400
in la which is amazing it could have been
much worse but yeah it was a tough time how

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:22.390
are you able to book a job at the end of all
that i asked for help it was so hard i my

00:03:22.390 --> 00:03:29.799
cousin told me a story um it's a short story
i can tell it quickly and people probably

00:03:29.799 --> 00:03:36.189
heard it before i forget all the details but
essentially there was a man in his house and

00:03:36.189 --> 00:03:42.930
it started raining and as the rain came down
it didn't stop and it started to flood and

00:03:42.930 --> 00:03:48.760
he was like oh my gosh how am i going to be
okay and he just kept praying to god god save

00:03:48.760 --> 00:03:55.319
me i will be okay please help me god and the
flood kept growing and the rain was getting

00:03:55.319 --> 00:03:59.370
worse and so he goes to the upstairs in his
house so he can get to the next level because

00:03:59.370 --> 00:04:07.349
it's starting to come into his house and he
sees um someone in their truck drives by and

00:04:07.349 --> 00:04:11.930
they're like hey man we can help you come
jump in the truck and he was like no god will

00:04:11.930 --> 00:04:18.780
save me i'm going to go upstairs to this upper
level i will be fine and then the rain keeps

00:04:18.780 --> 00:04:26.370
coming and the flood is rising and someone
in a boat passes by and they're like hey jump

00:04:26.370 --> 00:04:29.620
in the boat we'll help you we'll save you
they see him in this window you know jump

00:04:29.620 --> 00:04:35.949
out of your wind and he's like no i'll be
okay i'm praying god will save me and then

00:04:35.949 --> 00:04:40.020
finally it becomes like unbearable and he
has to literally climb to the roof of his

00:04:40.020 --> 00:04:48.120
house and a helicopter comes and flies over
and drops a ladder and says you know we grab

00:04:48.120 --> 00:04:54.130
the ladder we can save you i said no god will
save me i prayed and i know god will save

00:04:54.130 --> 00:05:03.590
me and the water rose so much that he couldn't
go anywhere and he ended up drowning and died

00:05:03.590 --> 00:05:08.930
and he goes to heaven and he's like god what
the heck i prayed for you to save me why didn't

00:05:08.930 --> 00:05:18.629
you save me and god said i sent you a truck
i sent you a boat i sent you a helicopter

00:05:18.629 --> 00:05:22.660
and when my cousin told me that story

00:05:22.660 --> 00:05:28.910
it really helped me see that like if you're
drowning it's okay to ask for help you know

00:05:28.910 --> 00:05:35.229
i felt very ashamed of where i was you know
in my life and how much i was struggling because

00:05:35.229 --> 00:05:40.099
again i really tied it to my self-worth and
i just thought you know i'm not a good enough

00:05:40.099 --> 00:05:44.550
human being i don't deserve this and so i
don't even deserve somebody's help you know

00:05:44.550 --> 00:05:50.740
and i would ghost people's texts you know
i just wouldn't even respond to friends texts

00:05:50.740 --> 00:05:55.810
or calls because i was so ashamed i was so
embarrassed you know if they say how are you

00:05:55.810 --> 00:05:59.650
what's going on and i would say nothing nine
months later and still nothing is going on

00:05:59.650 --> 00:06:09.220
you know i was so ashamed so after my cousin
told me that story i was like okay and so

00:06:09.220 --> 00:06:13.870
i reached out to one of the girls that i had
become friendly with from the first show and

00:06:13.870 --> 00:06:19.069
i told her look i really need a job if you
know of anyone looking because she was the

00:06:19.069 --> 00:06:22.190
same age as me but she had already been in
the industry longer and she knew a lot of

00:06:22.190 --> 00:06:26.560
people and so she had found other work i said
i really need a job if you hear of anything

00:06:26.560 --> 00:06:36.280
could you let me know and she heard of something
and she recommended me and it was glee and

00:06:36.280 --> 00:06:44.759
they called me and they called me a week after
i finally got a waitressing job a week after

00:06:44.759 --> 00:06:50.100
and i was like you have no idea how much i
want to come work for you but it was to fill

00:06:50.100 --> 00:06:57.389
in for somebody for just a couple days and
i was like you have no idea how badly i want

00:06:57.389 --> 00:07:02.319
to say yes to you but i just spent like eight
or nine months with no job and i just got

00:07:02.319 --> 00:07:06.949
this waitressing job a week ago i don't think
i can quit this for just a couple days work

00:07:06.949 --> 00:07:13.370
even though i really want to and so i said
like if something else comes up longer term

00:07:13.370 --> 00:07:20.389
please please call me and a month later they
did and they called me and they said now we

00:07:20.389 --> 00:07:25.439
need someone to fill in for the rest of the
season and i gave my notice after a month

00:07:25.439 --> 00:07:31.710
of waitressing and i went and worked on glee
wow because that goes back to that energy

00:07:31.710 --> 00:07:38.030
shift and so here you had something you finally
got a waitressing job after you know racing

00:07:38.030 --> 00:07:43.060
down to an open call for one and then then
this other one opened up so that goes back

00:07:43.060 --> 00:07:47.150
to what you're talking about earlier definitely
i mean even just getting the waitressing job

00:07:47.150 --> 00:07:53.400
it filled me up so much you know and not even
just self-worth and feeling like wow okay

00:07:53.400 --> 00:07:58.740
i finally have some money coming in i can
survive and and i have a job but also being

00:07:58.740 --> 00:08:02.419
around other people you know because i was
so afraid to spend money that i wouldn't leave

00:08:02.419 --> 00:08:08.340
my room i would stay in my room in my apartment
like all day every day i was afraid to leave

00:08:08.340 --> 00:08:13.159
because it would cost me money to leave and
i just i wasn't around people and so that

00:08:13.159 --> 00:08:18.439
i think also helped to be around people and
you know waitressing your your hustling and

00:08:18.439 --> 00:08:22.750
walking fast and something so i was getting
exercise again and moving my body you know

00:08:22.750 --> 00:08:28.409
all of these things that were beneficial that
i think really shifted my energy than to be

00:08:28.409 --> 00:08:37.840
open for a tv show very interesting wow what
a story so so when you got the waitressing

00:08:37.840 --> 00:08:44.030
job how did that feel because i realized that
wasn't your ultimate destination no it wasn't

00:08:44.030 --> 00:08:51.830
but i was elated you know i just needed something
i hadn't had any work in so long i i was so

00:08:51.830 --> 00:08:59.440
so happy and so grateful to get that waitressing
job yes wow do you ever go back to that restaurant

00:08:59.440 --> 00:09:04.040
well then know now you probably can't because
it's just takeout if it's still around right

00:09:04.040 --> 00:09:09.131
well so that so it's funny because so it's
on the block near where i live so i could

00:09:09.131 --> 00:09:18.140
walk there and i felt so guilty about like
leaving after just a month and i could tell

00:09:18.140 --> 00:09:23.330
the manager was not happy with me that on
glee i was an assistant and i was in charge

00:09:23.330 --> 00:09:28.090
of getting lunch every day so i would always
say hey we should go to this restaurant and

00:09:28.090 --> 00:09:31.550
so i gave them business and we would order
